## How to work in this project

1. Read `docs/Handsoff.md` end to end before changing anything.
2. Before the first slice, run `docs/Handsoff/bootstrap-sandbox.md` top to bottom. If this is a docs-only greenfield repo, that bootstrap creates the bare Laravel scaffold first. Do not implement slices until it prints the sandbox URL and writes `docs/larv/07-runtime/sandbox-url.txt`.
3. Read `STATE.yaml.execution.review_mode`. If it is `auto-all`, continue slice-by-slice until the app is fully tested or a blocker appears. If it is `manual-slice`, `manual-adr`, or `manual-phase`, pause at that boundary with the public URL and exact QA checklist before continuing.
4. To implement a slice, open the matching `docs/Handsoff/slice-NN-<name>.md` and execute it top to bottom.
5. Probe-before-announce: never tell the user a URL until you have curl-confirmed the service responds.
6. Recommendation-with-every-question: when you ask the user a question, pair it with a recommendation block:
   ```
   Recommendation: <option>
   Why: <one-sentence reason citing the plan>
   Tradeoffs: <one-line counterpoint>
   ```
7. After every change:
   - Append a tracker entry (snippet in the slice handsoff).
   - Update `STATE.yaml.slices.<slice-id>.status` (snippet in the slice handsoff).
   - Write the implementation report under `docs/larv/08-implementation/reports/`.
   - If UI changed, make it match `docs/larv/03-design/visual-implementation-contract.md` and the chosen mockups, save desktop/mobile screenshots under `docs/larv/08-implementation/screenshots/`, and record reference paths plus any approved deviations.
   - Verify source code against the PRD/product brief and `docs/larv` plan. Fix missing features, contradictions, incomplete workflows, weak seed data, missing tests, and undocumented scope cuts before reporting completion.
   - Keep one coherent app layout/design system for all roles. Prefer the policy-app pattern: admin routes use the same main app shell/layout/sidebar as normal routes, while admin menu groups/actions are conditionally visible by role. If Filament/admin features are used, admin resources and menus must be shown or hidden by authorization inside the shared layout, not implemented as a separate-looking admin app.
   - Write or update the per-slice guide in `docs/user-manual/testing/`.
   - Update `docs/user-manual/seed-data.md` with seeded records, test credentials for every role/persona needed for full-flow QA, reset commands, and test-data removal notes.
   - Do not add a navbar impersonation/user-switching UI unless the product requirements explicitly ask for one. Reviewers must use normal login with seeded credentials.
   - Run required Laravel commands yourself: migrations, seeders, cache clears, asset builds, queue/runtime restarts, package-specific commands, and sandbox restarts/probes when code changes require them. Do not ask the user to run sandbox commands manually.
   - Keep the sandbox current after app code changes and show the user the public URL from `docs/larv/07-runtime/sandbox-url.txt` only after probing it.
   - Append to `docs/larv/local-learnings.md` if you discovered something.
   - Run the slice's Definition of Done checklist before claiming completion.
   - Identify yourself accurately when filling `created_by.tool` and `created_by.model` in the tracker.

## Fresh session recovery

When opening a new AI session because context is high or the previous session ended:

1. Read `DOCS.md`.
2. Read `docs/Handsoff.md`.
3. Read `docs/larv/STATE.yaml`.
4. Read `docs/larv/implementation-tracker.yaml` and `docs/larv/implementation-tracker.md` if present.
5. Read the latest reports in `docs/larv/08-implementation/reports/` if they exist.
6. Read `docs/larv/07-runtime/sandbox-url.txt` if it exists. If it is missing, run `docs/Handsoff/bootstrap-sandbox.md` before app code edits; it handles missing `artisan` by scaffolding Laravel first.
7. Read `docs/user-manual/README.md` and `docs/user-manual/seed-data.md`.
8. Find the first slice whose STATE status is not completed.
9. Open the matching `docs/Handsoff/slice-NN-<name>.md` and continue from its next unchecked Definition of Done item.
10. If all slices are complete, run verification or deployment depending on the user's request.

If larv commands are available, `/larv:resume` can do the same state lookup for a managed project.

## Model switch starter prompt

Before continuing in a new AI tool or fresh session, switch to the user's preferred model. If no preference was given, use the strongest coding model available in this tool for long-context Laravel implementation.

## Model split: planning vs execution

- **Planning** (brainstorm, design, ADRs, slice writing, premortem, handoff generation, debugging hard architectural issues): use **Opus**. Planning needs the strongest reasoning.
- **Execution** (implementing an already-written slice, running tests, applying lints, mechanical edits, doc updates, sandbox restarts, screenshot capture): use a **cheaper/faster model** — Sonnet, Haiku, or Codex CLI. Execution should burn the lighter model so Opus context stays available for the next planning pass.
- When you (Opus) finish a planning artifact, hand it off explicitly: tell the user the next step is execution on Sonnet/Haiku/Codex and stop. Do not continue executing the slice yourself unless the user asks.
- When you (Sonnet/Haiku/Codex) hit a real planning question — ambiguous slice, missing ADR, contradicting plan — stop and tell the user to switch back to Opus. Do not improvise plan-level decisions.

Starter prompt:

```text
You are continuing a larv-managed Laravel implementation in this same repository. Do not rely on previous chat context. Start by reading DOCS.md, docs/Handsoff.md, docs/larv/STATE.yaml, docs/larv/implementation-tracker.yaml if present, and the latest docs/larv/08-implementation/reports/. If docs/larv/07-runtime/sandbox-url.txt is missing, run docs/Handsoff/bootstrap-sandbox.md before app code edits. Find the first incomplete slice in STATE, open the matching docs/Handsoff/slice-NN-<name>.md, execute its instructions, run required Laravel/frontend commands yourself, restart/probe the sandbox when needed, and report the public URL plus the testing guide path. Do not SSH; this session is already inside the VM.
```

## New feature requests

When the user asks for a new feature in a managed project:

1. If larv commands are available, run `/larv:feature <feature-name>`.
2. If larv commands are not available, read `docs/Handsoff.md`, `docs/larv/STATE.yaml`, `docs/larv/implementation-tracker.yaml`, `adr/`, and `docs/larv/01-domain/`.
3. Create `docs/larv/features/<feature-slug>/` with the feature brief, domain delta, design delta, test delta, premortem, and implementation slices.
4. Append tracker entries with `type: feature`.

## Code conventions

- Format: Pint (`vendor/bin/pint`)
- Tests: Pest (`vendor/bin/pest`)
- Static analysis: Larastan (`vendor/bin/phpstan analyse`)
- Use the canonical ubiquitous-language terms from `docs/larv/01-domain/ubiquitous-language.md`. Do not invent new names for existing concepts.

